course: dictionary
lesson: 6088
title: Three names for one space rock
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A meteoroid is a small rock or bit of metal travelling through space, and it is smaller than an asteroid. «A space rock is a meteoroid in space» tells you the key idea: the rock keeps this name while it is out in space.
explain: The name changes when the rock reaches our air. It starts to glow and burn up, and the streak of light it makes is a meteor, also called a «shooting star». The clue words «glows» and «burns up» show you that a meteor is the light, not the rock.
explain: If a piece survives the burning and lands on the ground, it is called a meteorite. Remember the three words in order: meteoroid in space, meteor in the air, meteorite on the ground. Say «meteoroid, meteor and meteorite» aloud to help you remember them.
fact: A meteoroid is a small rock or bit of metal travelling through space.
fact: A meteoroid is smaller than an asteroid.
fact: A meteor is the streak of light made when a space rock burns up in our air.
fact: A meteor is also called a shooting star.
fact: «A meteorite is a piece that reaches the ground.»
exercise: Where is a meteoroid while it is still a meteoroid? | *In space | On the ground | Inside a volcano | At the bottom of the sea
exercise: «The space rock glowed as it burned up in our air.» What is it called at that moment? | An asteroid | *A meteor | A meteoroid | A meteorite
exercise: «A piece of the rock reached the ground.» What is that piece called? | A meteor | *A meteorite | A meteoroid | A small planet
test: Which name is given to a piece of space rock that lands on the ground? | Comet | *Meteorite | Meteoroid | Meteor
test: A space rock travels from space, burns in the air, then lands. Which order of names is right? | Meteoroid, meteorite, meteor | Meteor, meteorite, meteoroid | *Meteoroid, meteor, meteorite | Meteorite, meteor, meteoroid
exam: What is a meteor? | A very large planet | A meteoroid that is still out in space | A rock that has landed on Earth | *The streak of light made when a space rock burns up in our air
